// Your overrides of what Dynamapper's paperdolls take off beneath a robe: the 3D paperdoll of the Enhanced and KR-like
// presets, and the gump paperdolls (the Classic preset, and the old-style paperdoll setting). The uocf editor keeps the
// client's rules (WearableRules.kdl).
//
// The client draws the shirt, trousers, skirt, sleeves and chest pieces beneath a robe or dress, and their 3D frames do
// not line up with the robe's: their edges show past it. When paperdoll_ec.uddp is packed, udd-cli (or the converter
// GUI) measures every dress-layer item on each player body (human, elf and gargoyle, male and female): it draws each
// garment of the slots torso, legs, skirt, arms, chest and abovechest the client puts beneath the item, with and
// without it. When what vanishes lies within 16 texels of the item's edges (fewer than 1,600 texels beyond), the
// garment is taken off under that item on that body; otherwise it is in plain view and stays. The package stores the
// garments each item keeps on each body; the packing log lists them, one line per item and body, and
// `pack-ec-paperdoll --report <file>` (the GUI's Findings Report) writes every measurement. Only what the client draws
// beneath the item is taken off, and only the garments the packer could measure (with a tileart Layer and a 3D frame
// for the body); a taken-off item counts as not worn, so the client's rules it would set off, as plate legs hiding the
// shoes, do not apply. The gump paperdolls take the decision for the 3D body of their profile, and
// only while paperdoll_ec.uddp is loaded; the gump art shows the edges of armour past a robe as well.
//
// This file ships empty. Dynamapper reads your copy, cache/settings/PaperdollOverrides.kdl beside the assets folder,
// in its place when it exists. The paperdoll windows write that copy: right-click a garment drawn beneath a robe or
// dress for "Show under this robe", "Hide under this robe" or "Back to measured"; the same menu lists the garments
// taken off beneath the robe, each to be brought back. A choice is for that robe on that body: it edits the cover
// `cover "dress" items="<robe>" bodies="<body>"` headed "chosen in the paperdoll window" (added at the end when
// there is none), keeping your comments and other covers as written; "Back to measured" also takes the garment out
// of your other covers that apply to that robe and body. The first choice starts the copy from this file. A copy
// that does not read is neither read nor written, with a warning, and this file applies until it is fixed.
//
// A cover decides for its covering items, over the stored decisions:
//
// cover "<slot>" items="any"|"<ids>" [bodies="<body frames>"] {
//     hide "<slot>"; hide items="<ids>"; show items="<ids>"; except items="<ids>"
// }
//   While an item of the covering slot among `items` is drawn on one of `bodies` (the number of the 3D body frame
//   drawn: 400 and 401 human, 605 and 606 elf, 666 and 667 gargoyle, male then female; every body without `bodies`),
//   the cover decides what is taken off beneath it, and only among what the client draws there:
//   - Garment rules: `show items=` keeps those garments, `hide items=` takes them off. One cover may not do both to a
//     garment.
//   - Slot rules: `hide "<slot>"` takes off the garments of that slot no garment rule names. A cover with no rule at
//     all keeps everything beneath its items, as the client draws it; a cover with garment rules alone leaves the other
//     garments to the covers before it and the stored decision.
//   - `except` lists covering items the cover leaves alone.
//   Precedence: garment rules beat slot rules, which beat the stored decision. Among the covers that apply, the last
//   one in the file with a garment rule naming the garment decides it; else the last one with a slot rule (or none at
//   all) decides; else the stored decision does. Item ids are decimal or 0x hex; slots are those of WearableRules.kdl.
//   Unlike the stored decisions, a slot rule also takes off the garments the packer could not measure. A cover on
//   another slot than "dress" adds a rule the package has none of. `udd-cli tool export-kdl paperdoll_ec.uddp` (the
//   converter GUI's Tools tab, Export Paperdoll Overrides) writes the decisions a package stores in this syntax.
//
// Examples:
//
// The tabard keeps most shirts beneath it (the stored decision: they show around it); take them off anyway:
//   cover "dress" items="0xA412" {
//       hide "torso"
//   }
//
// Keep the fancy shirt beneath the plain robe on the human male, and take the doublet off there:
//   cover "dress" items="0x1F03" bodies="400" {
//       show items="0x1EFD"
//       hide items="0x1F7B"
//   }
//
// Draw every robe as the client draws it:
//   cover "dress" items="any"
